      article.main
        h1 Template Inheritance
        p
          | Pug supports template inheritance. Template inheritance works via the
          code block
          | and
          code extends
          | keywords.
        p
          | In a template, a
          code block
          | is simply a “block” of Pug that a child template may replace. This process is recursive.
        p
          | Pug blocks can provide default content, if appropriate. Providing default content is purely optional, though. The example below defines
          code block scripts
          | ,
          code block content
          | , and
          code block foot
          | .
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- layout.pug
            span.cm-tag html
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag head
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag title
            span.cm-string My Site - #{title}
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable scripts
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/jquery.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag body
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable content
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable foot
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-builtin #footer
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag p
            span.cm-string some footer content
        p
          | To extend this layout, create a new file and use the
          code extends
          | directive with a path to the parent template. (If no file extension is given,
          code .pug
          | is automatically appended to the file name.) Then, define one or more blocks to override the parent block content.
        p
          | Below, notice that the
          code foot
          | block is
          em not
          | redefined, so it will use the parent’s default and output “some footer content”.
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- page-a.pug
            span.cm-keyword extends
            span.cm-string layout.pug
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable scripts
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/jquery.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/pets.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable content
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag h1
            span.cm-punctuation =
            span.cm-variable title
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-punctuation -
            span.cm-keyword var
            span.cm-def pets
            span.cm-operator =
            | [
            span.cm-string 'cat'
            | ,
            span.cm-string 'dog'
            | ]
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword each
            span.cm-variable petName
            span.cm-keyword in
            span.cm-variable pets
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword include
            span.cm-string pet.pug
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- pet.pug
            span.cm-tag p
            span.cm-punctuation =
            span.cm-variable petName
        p
          | It’s also possible to override a block to provide additional blocks, as shown in the following example. As it shows,
          code content
          | now exposes a
          code sidebar
          | and
          code primary
          | block for overriding. (Alternatively, the child template could override
          code content
          | altogether.)
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- sub-layout.pug
            span.cm-keyword extends
            span.cm-string layout.pug
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable content
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-qualifier .sidebar
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable sidebar
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag p
            span.cm-string nothing
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-qualifier .primary
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable primary
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag p
            span.cm-string nothing
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- page-b.pug
            span.cm-keyword extends
            span.cm-string sub-layout.pug
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable content
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-qualifier .sidebar
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable sidebar
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag p
            span.cm-string nothing
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-qualifier .primary
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable primary
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag p
            span.cm-string nothing
        h2#block-append-prepend
          | Block
          code append
          | /
          code prepend
          a.header-anchor(href="#block-append-prepend" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p
          | Pug allows you to
          code replace
          | (default),
          code prepend
          | , or
          code append
          | blocks.
        p
          | Suppose you have default scripts in a
          code head
          | block that you wish to use on
          em every
          | page. You might do this:
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- layout.pug
            span.cm-tag html
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag head
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable head
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/vendor/jquery.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/vendor/caustic.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag body
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-keyword block
            span.cm-variable content
        p
          | Now, consider a page of your JavaScript game. You want some game related scripts as well as these defaults. You can simply
          code append
          | the block:
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- page.pug
            span.cm-keyword extends
            span.cm-string layout.pug
            span.cm-keyword block append
            span.cm-variable head
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/vendor/three.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/game.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
        p
          | When using
          code block append
          | or
          code block prepend
          | , the word “
          code block
          | ” is optional:
        pre.cm-s-default
          code
            span.cm-comment //- page.pug
            span.cm-keyword extends
            span.cm-string layout
            span.cm-keyword append
            span.cm-variable head
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/vendor/three.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
            span.cm-indent
            span.cm-tag script
            span.cm-punctuation (
            span.cm-attribute src
            span.cm-operator =
            span.cm-string '/game.js'
            span.cm-punctuation )
        h2#common-mistakes
          | Common mistakes
          a.header-anchor(href="#common-mistakes" aria-hidden="true") ¶
        p Pug’s template inheritance is a powerful feature that allows you to split complex page template structures into smaller, simpler files. However, if you chain many, many templates together, you can make things a lot more complicated for yourself.
        p
          | Note that
          strong only named blocks and mixin definitions
          | can appear at the top (unindented) level of a child template. This is important! Parent templates define a page’s overall structure, and child templates can only
          code append
          | ,
          code prepend
          | , or replace specific blocks of markup and logic. If a child template tried to add content outside of a block, Pug would have no way of knowing where to put it in the final page.
        p
          | This includes
          a(href="code.html#unbuffered-code") unbuffered code
          | , which can also contain markup. If you need to define variables for use in a child template, you can do so a few different ways:
        ul
          li
            | Add the variables to the Pug
            a(href="../api/reference.html#options") options
            | object, or define them in unbuffered code in a parent template. The child template will inherit these variables.
          li
            | Define the variables
            em in a block
            | in the child template. Extending templates must have at least one block, or it would be empty — just define your variables there.
        p
          | For the same reason, Pug’s
          a(href="comments.html") buffered comments
          | cannot appear at the top level of an extending template: they produce HTML comments which would have nowhere to go in the resulting HTML. (Unbuffered Pug comments, however, can still go anywhere.)
        div(style="margin-top: 50px")
